WEBVTT
TITLE Graphs Connectivity — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
A graph is two sets: V, the vertices, and E, a set of unordered pairs of vertices — the edges.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Two representations: a matrix whose rows are vertices and whose 1s mark edges, or a list of neighbours for each vertex.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.000
The degree of a vertex is the number of edges at it — the row sum of the matrix, the length of the list.

4
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Every number on this page is computed from those two sets, never read off the drawing.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Add up all the degrees and every edge has been counted twice, once at each end. The sum is twice the number of edges.

6
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:46.000
So the degree sum is always even, and the vertices of odd degree must come in pairs.

7
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Here the degrees 2, 3, 3, 2, 2 sum to 12, so there are exactly 6 edges — and vertices 1 and 2 are the two odd ones.

8
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.000
The lemma rules things out: you cannot have a graph with exactly one vertex of odd degree.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.000
A walk may repeat vertices and edges; a path repeats neither. A cycle is a closed path of length at least three.

10
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:16.000
A graph is connected when a path joins every pair of vertices. A component is a maximal connected piece.

11
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.000
This graph has three: the triangle, the two-vertex path, and vertex 5 alone. An isolated vertex is its own component.

12
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Distance is the length of the shortest path, and it is defined only inside one component.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:38.000
A tree is a connected graph with no cycle. Three statements then say the same thing about a graph on n vertices.

14
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Connected and acyclic; exactly n − 1 edges; and exactly one path between every pair of vertices.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Each is a test you can run: count the edges, look for a cycle, or ask whether two routes exist.

16
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Adding any edge to a tree creates exactly one cycle, and deleting any edge disconnects it.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Every connected graph has a spanning tree: a tree on all the vertices, using some of the edges.

18
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Find a cycle, delete one of its edges, and repeat. Each deletion keeps the graph connected and removes a cycle, so the process must stop — at a tree.

19
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.000
K4 has 16 spanning trees. Cayley says a complete graph on n vertices has n to the power n − 2, which is 4 squared here.

20
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Two independent computations of the same number, which is the only reason to trust either.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.000
A cut vertex is a vertex whose removal increases the number of components. A bridge is an edge with the same property.

22
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:46.000
They are different objects. This graph has two cut vertices, 2 and 5, and one bridge — the edge between them.

23
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:53.000
A bridge’s endpoints are cut vertices only when they have degree at least two.

24
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
No cycle has either, because every edge of a cycle sits on an alternative route.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.000
How connected is a connected graph? Count the fewest things you must delete to break it.

26
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Vertex connectivity kappa deletes vertices; edge connectivity lambda deletes edges; delta is the smallest degree.

27
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Whitney’s inequality: kappa is at most lambda, and lambda is at most delta.

28
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Here kappa and lambda are 1 because of the bridge, while delta is 2. The chain bounds the answer; it does not fix it.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.000
A graph is bipartite when the vertices split into two sides and every edge crosses between them.

30
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Equivalently: colour one side 0 and the other 1, so every edge joins different colours. That is a 2-colouring.

31
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:53.000
The obstruction is an odd cycle. Walking a 5-cycle forces alternating colours, and the last edge meets its own colour.

32
00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:00.000
So bipartite is the same as no odd cycle, and a breadth-first search decides it by parity of distance.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
An Euler circuit uses every edge exactly once and returns to the start. A Hamiltonian cycle visits every vertex exactly once.

34
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Euler has a one-line test: every edge in one component and every degree even. Zero odd vertices gives a circuit, two gives a trail that is not closed.

35
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Hamilton has no such test. No simple necessary-and-sufficient condition is known, and deciding it is NP-complete.

36
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
They are independent: the two-triangle graph has an Euler trail but no Hamiltonian cycle; K4 minus an edge has a Hamiltonian cycle but no Euler circuit.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.000
The procedure: count vertices and edges, check the degrees, find the components, then ask what the question wants broken.

38
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Trap one: reading the picture instead of the structure. Two drawings of the same graph look different and answer the same.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Trap two: confusing a cut vertex with a bridge, or Euler with Hamilton. Each pair counts different objects.

40
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Trap three: applying a criterion outside its condition. The all-even-degrees test needs every edge in one component.

